ath12k: ahb: let remoteproc own the user PD when it already does

On IPQ5332 the multipd remoteproc driver can be told to register an
rproc per user PD, which is how the vendor device tree describes this
hardware: its wifi node points qcom,rproc at the user PD itself. This
driver instead treats qcom,rproc as the root PD and then loads, PAS
authenticates and spawns the user PD on its own.

Both cannot be true at once. With the vendor DT shape the PD boots and
this driver then cannot have the interrupt it needs:

  remoteproc remoteproc2: remote processor pd1 is now up
  genirq: Flags mismatch irq 31. 00002001 (UserPD1-spawn)
          vs. 00002001 (q6v5_wcss_userpd1_spawn-ack)
  ath12k_ahb c000000.wifi: error -EBUSY: Request spawn irq failed: -16

Doing the load here instead does not work with vendor firmware either.
The images are linked for a single loader filling one region --
q6_fw0 up to 0x4ab51000, q6_fw3 at 0x4aefd000, q6_fw1 at 0x4b2a3000 --
so q6_fw1 falls below the per-PD window at 0x4bd00000 and the load
fails as "segment outside memory range", while widening the window to
the whole Q6 region lets it succeed and then kills the running root PD.

Detect the case from the device tree rather than adding a new property:
a user PD node is the one carrying a "spawn" smem state. When
qcom,rproc resolves to such a node, skip the interrupt request, the
image load and the shutdown handshake, and let remoteproc drive the PD.

This is a local patch. Upstream still has to decide which of the two
models is intended; see the discussion on linux-remoteproc.

Signed-off-by: Hideo Sumi <hideo.sumi@mugops.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/ahb.c
@@ -338,6 +338,32 @@
 	ath12k_ce_cleanup_pipes(ab);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The multipd remoteproc driver creates a device per user PD, and a user PD
+ * node is the one carrying the "spawn" smem state. When qcom,rproc resolves
+ * to such a node, remoteproc already owns the image, the PAS authentication
+ * and the spawn handshake, so this driver must not do any of it: both of
+ * them requesting the same spawn interrupt fails the second requester with
+ * -EBUSY, and the vendor firmware is in any case linked for a single loader
+ * filling one region, which this driver cannot reproduce from its own
+ * per-PD memory window.
+ */
+static bool ath12k_ahb_rproc_owns_userpd(struct ath12k_base *ab)
+{
+	struct ath12k_ahb *ab_ahb = ath12k_ab_to_ahb(ab);
+	struct device *rproc_dev;
+
+	if (!ab_ahb->tgt_rproc)
+		return false;
+
+	rproc_dev = ab_ahb->tgt_rproc->dev.parent;
+	if (!rproc_dev || !rproc_dev->of_node)
+		return false;
+
+	return of_property_match_string(rproc_dev->of_node,
+					"qcom,smem-state-names", "spawn") >= 0;
+}
+
 static int ath12k_ahb_power_up(struct ath12k_base *ab)
 {
 	struct ath12k_ahb *ab_ahb = ath12k_ab_to_ahb(ab);
@@ -353,6 +379,12 @@
 	u32 pasid;
 	int ret;
 
+	if (ath12k_ahb_rproc_owns_userpd(ab)) {
+		ath12k_dbg(ab, ATH12K_DBG_AHB,
+			   "user PD owned by remoteproc, skipping local boot\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	rmem = ath12k_core_get_reserved_mem(ab, 0);
 	if (!rmem)
 		return -ENODEV;
@@ -470,6 +502,9 @@
 	u32 pasid;
 	int ret;
 
+	if (ath12k_ahb_rproc_owns_userpd(ab))
+		return;
+
 	qcom_smem_state_update_bits(ab_ahb->stop_state, BIT(ab_ahb->stop_bit),
 				    BIT(ab_ahb->stop_bit));
 
@@ -889,6 +924,9 @@
 		}
 	}
 
+	if (ath12k_ahb_rproc_owns_userpd(ab))
+		return 0;
+
 	return ath12k_ahb_config_rproc_irq(ab);
 
 err_unreg_notifier:
